  overview: "Patellar tendon rupture is a failure of the knee extensor mechanism just below the patella. It is rarer than quadriceps tendon rupture, with an incidence of 0.68 per 100,000, and it usually affects younger, physically active people, often with pre-existing tendon pathology [r1]. In NFL players the most common mechanism was an eccentric overload to a contracting extensor mechanism, a palpable defect was present in all 24 ruptures, and 11 of 24 had antecedent symptoms [r2]. A high-riding patella (patella alta) on a lateral radiograph is the hallmark sign, described in a small paediatric series [r3].\n\nComplete ruptures need surgical repair, and optimal results typically occur when the tear is repaired acutely [r4]. A review of 20 studies (1,161 patients) found that suture anchor, transosseous and end-to-end repairs all achieved high healing rates [r4]. Across 9 studies of acute repair, the overall re-rupture rate was 3.9% and the re-operation rate 5.7% [r5]. The evidence is observational. Augmentation is debated: steel wire cerclage gave the lowest Lysholm scores in one review [r6], whereas hamstring autograft augmentation gave better return to sport than steel wire cerclage in a multicentre cohort [r7].\n\nReturn to sport is usually possible but not guaranteed. In a systematic review, 88.9% of patients returned to play [r8], but only 56.8% of NFL players with extensor mechanism ruptures did, at a median of 11.3 months [r9].",

