    text: "The patellar tendon (strictly a ligament, but called a tendon clinically) joins the inferior pole of the patella to the tibial tuberosity and continues the quadriceps tendon over the front of the patella. It transmits quadriceps force to the tibia, and it holds the patella at its normal height, which is assessed on a lateral radiograph with the Insall–Salvati ratio [r3]. The medial and lateral patellar retinacula run alongside, and the infrapatellar fat pad and deep infrapatellar bursa lie behind it. In adolescents the tibial tuberosity is an apophysis, so the distal attachment fails differently in a growing knee [r3].",
